Compared to calibration anchors: Papers with strong theoretical offline RL contributions accepted at ICLR scored 7-8 (e.g., tVMPfEGT2w: scores 8, 8, 6, 8). Papers with automata/formal language theory in RL settings scored in the 5-6 range (e.g., Oz9FTPINRe: 6, 5, 6, 6; 73lu1yw6At: 5, 6, 6, 6). This paper has strong theoretical novelty (exponential gap proof, unification of metrics, correction of prior work) that exceeds the typical paper in this space, but the missing primary baseline and the conditional nature of the PAC guarantee on an unverified hyperparameter pull it down. The core theoretical claims are sound, the proof appears correct, and the experiments confirm the scaling predictions. Relative to the anchors, this sits above the typical 5-6 automata+RL papers due to the rigor of the sample complexity analysis, but below the 7-8 papers due to the experimental gaps.

