This paper sits squarely in the 5–6 range. It has a genuinely novel pipeline and meaningful empirical findings (the GPT-2 > GPT-3.5 prompting result is interesting; the RL vs. BC gap is clear). However, it lacks objective validation of task success, does not compare against modern preference optimization baselines, and relies entirely on a small subjective user study — concerns that directly mirror those in the exact-match human review. The paper is better than the low-scoring anchors (which had fundamental flaws or misleading claims) but falls short of the high-scoring dialog/RL papers (which had stronger, more comprehensive evaluations).

